[Music]  this  is ohsu  shaped by leaders pioneers  and those brave enough to take that  first step  we carry the torch and shine a light on  what is possible  we are a collaboration of disciplines  listening to one another learning from  each other  the ohsu clinical informatics phd  program  is more than an opportunity to gain  experience  it's your time to serve with a community  of trailblazers  and groundbreakers i learned about the  field  of informatics as i was finishing my  training in internal medicine  and i thought this is for me it combines  medicine it combines computers and  information  as soon as i got into it and started  doing the work i realized that this was  my calling in life this this combination  of medicine and information like  most phd programs you typically do a  dissertation where you focus in  on a specific area of research and  oftentimes you take that area of  research with you into the work that you  perform  and so the goal really is to become a  researcher a thought leader  in informatics our phd program  is one of 16 around the country that's  funded by the national library medicine  which is the part of the national  institutes of health  that funds research training in  biomedical informatics  part of the challenge with informatics  is that  there's so many aspects usually to any  project that you need someone who really  understands electronic health records  and you need somebody else who really  understands  information retrieval and you need  someone who understands  how to make the systems talk to each  other it's  not a solo kind of area we're looking  for people that have kind of a natural  research curiosity  someone who is self-driven the win-win  situation is when a student comes in and  says gosh karen i'm really interested in  this area and i can say  gee you should go talk to professor x a  lot of my work is around like  image-based  informatics doing stuff with a disease  called retinopathy or prematurity  everyone here is very passionate about  what they do  um it's it's pretty remarkable actually  you learn a lot obviously but then  there's also like a  very like personal connection with  people too that you kind of build and  you can tell the people are are there  not just to get you through the program  but they're there for you  right now i'm working on a project um  where i'm working with pulmonology in  the cystic fibrosis  adult care center to learn more about  the ways that wildfire smoke affects  the health of people with cystic  fibrosis  i'm also working with the department of  family medicine  on a research project that's looking at  social determinants of health  julie i found ohsu to be like a really  great place  if you're an intellectually curious  person where it very much rewards  intellectual curiosity and where there's  lots of people who want  to teach you things and want to give you  those opportunities  you get in here and everyone has  different backgrounds so i personally  had a biochemistry background i know  people have  biology backgrounds there are people  with math backgrounds computer science  backgrounds  so it's funny where we'll get into a  class you know that's maybe  centered around computer science or  something like that and those people  seem to excel and the rest of us are  like oh what is all this you know but  then you get into a  class that's more biology related and  you start excelling in the  the cs people are like ah what is this  you know so  it's fun like because then you can work  together and you can all  help each other and teach each other  along the way all the students  do at least two research rotations with  different faculty members  so that they get a chance to learn hey  do i like working with karen  do i like the area of research maybe i  like to work  with paul or maybe i like to work with  joan so it gives them a chance to  learn what the other faculty members are  doing in terms of research  i would consider what sort of academic  environment you want to be in  it's a great program if you  have a lot of your own motivation and  something that you're willing  to pursue i don't think that you need to  be a tech geek at all  in fact i was interested in technology  but i didn't know anything about  programming  before i came here and i've learned so  much and i love it now  ohsu is a very collaborative community i  think that  one of the strengths of our institution  is that there is  a lot of common mission and mutual  respect for each other whether you're a  clinician  or a researcher or an educator or  someone who's working in informatics who  may be part  of any of those groups what they tell  you in like the open house formats or  anything like that  it's pretty candid you'll hear from us i  don't think there's  there aren't any secrets that you know  we're holding back or being  hushed up about it's it's a phd program  it's  if you're passionate about it you're  gonna do great uh it's not easy at all  times but  it's yeah it's where you wanna be then  you should be here  at some point it's about the why you do  what you do  as much as the how  we do it because we are bold  we do it because someone needs to make  the difference  and we know we can make the difference  because we already  have we welcome the curious  inspired and those willing to be bold  this is ohsu

